Lab 13: Compute Node Operations in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install and configure Nova compute services on a node.
Manage the lifecycle of instances (VMs) on compute nodes.
Monitor resource utilization on compute nodes.
Prerequisites
A running OpenStack environment (e.g., DevStack or manually deployed OpenStack).
SSH access to the compute node(s).
Basic familiarity with Linux command line and OpenStack CLI.
Python 3.x installed.
Task 1: Install and Configure Nova Compute Services
Subtask 1.1: Install Nova Compute Service
SSH into the compute node:
ssh username@compute_node_ip
Update the system and install Nova compute packages:
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install nova-compute -y
Verify the installation:
nova-compute --version
Expected Output: Version number of Nova compute (e.g., 23.0.0).
Subtask 1.2: Configure Nova Compute
Edit the Nova configuration file:
sudo nano /etc/nova/nova.conf
Update the following sections:
[DEFAULT]
compute_driver = libvirt.LibvirtDriver
[libvirt]
virt_type = kvm
Restart Nova services:
sudo systemctl restart nova-compute
sudo systemctl enable nova-compute
Verify service status:
sudo systemctl status nova-compute
Expected Output: active (running).
Task 2: Manage Instance Lifecycle on Compute Nodes
Subtask 2.1: Launch an Instance
Source OpenStack credentials:
source ~/openrc
Create a test instance:
openstack server create --flavor m1.small --image cirros --network private test-instance
Check instance status:
openstack server list
Expected Output: test-instance should be in ACTIVE state.
Subtask 2.2: Stop and Start an Instance
Stop the instance:
openstack server stop test-instance
Verify shutdown:
openstack server list
Expected Output: test-instance should be in SHUTOFF state.
Start the instance again:
openstack server start test-instance
Expected Output: Instance returns to ACTIVE state.
Subtask 2.3: Delete an Instance
Terminate the instance:
openstack server delete test-instance
Verify deletion:
openstack server list
Expected Output: test-instance should no longer appear.
Task 3: Monitor Resource Utilization
Subtask 3.1: Check Compute Node Resources
View compute node stats:
openstack compute service list
Expected Output: Lists all compute nodes with status (up or down).
Check resource usage:
openstack hypervisor stats show
Expected Output: Displays CPU, RAM, and disk usage.
Subtask 3.2: Monitor Instance Performance
List all instances on the compute node:
openstack server list --host compute_node_name
Check CPU and memory usage per instance:
openstack server show test-instance -f json | grep -E "cpu|memory"
Expected Output: Displays allocated CPU and memory.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:
✔ Installed and configured Nova compute services.
✔ Managed the lifecycle of instances (launch, stopping, starting, deleting).
✔ Monitored compute node resource utilization.

By completing these tasks, you now have hands-on experience in managing OpenStack compute nodes effectively.

Troubleshooting Tips
If Nova services fail to start, check logs:
journalctl -u nova-compute -f
If instances fail to launch, verify network and image availability.
Ensure KVM is supported on the host:
egrep -c '(vmx|svm)' /proc/cpuinfo
(Output should be > 0).
